alue "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192330} {:answer/id #uuid "437fe653-fd9f-53eb-9e04-17a88b8d4bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192331} {:answer/id #uuid "4e170e19-e1b1-5e95-b461-a4cb6c8f7ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "beb8e572-d355-5b9e-9db7-fad6bf9cc428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192328} :answer-field/id #uuid "b941308d-d487-55e0-9a91-cc64a7bfa6a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6fdbb6b-bf50-55db-8f1b-9ba9143d4ac9" :question/math-academy-id "q-90393" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[3X-4].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{1}{4},\\,2<x<6\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[3X-4]=3\\text{E}[X]-4\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}x\\cdot (\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8})|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-(-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[3X-4] & =3\\text{E}[X]-4 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{14}{3}-4 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198012 :question/id #uuid "f794d50a-cb44-5a6f-a922-8438a6192d46" :question/math-academy-id "e-11157" :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y)\\to(u(x,y), v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n![](images/b5/b52614b0fbaccee13747bedb98617811af7a4b13e64e14f761a16e6b274513ed.svg)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/5e/5e8faca7bd98b1af626603c26aef5fc4d7a65064f4cdc3486670195fc29dff65.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ and $v(x,y) = e^{x-y}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & ={e}^{x-y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x+y}\\cdot (-{e}^{x-y})-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y}-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198019 :question/id #uuid "4eaf3262-b6e3-57bc-9662-42ab52626ee9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12340" :question/problem "![](images/65/6515550b9a280f423cdc2a2e89fd83014733c0783fdd4b2b2966e6a4dd37492a.png)\n\n     \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}  \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\bigg\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/79/79869cf64ec827934397588e25d06cba0c9d12c35ecbea5cdd6aaf6cfcf12e24.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198026 :question/id #uuid "f4539253-b127-5d96-a331-3b357dc08a9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12363" :question/problem "![](images/86/86a240b5c7c1fc945e12da274be7e9cac14c1b5a9add004d52d72390afb39aff.png)\n\n     \n\nConsider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nDetermine the image of the top side of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),\\,1\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis and whose endpoints lie on circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/81/81650cc0dc43537ec916f2c68af66ed5581c861812f276dda2b23baccc2fb491.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198036 :question/id #uuid "5a596157-d6dd-528d-912c-52889780567d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12390" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle  \\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2} \\int_{1}^{4} \\dfrac{\\sin{y}}{x} \\: \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the domain of integration $R = [1,2] \\times \\left[0,\\pi/2\\right] \\times [1,4]$ is a rectangular box and the integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{dx}{x}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy{\\int }_{1}^{4}dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}\\,dx{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}dz & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{1}^{2}\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0)\\cdot (0+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2} \\int_{1}^{4} \\dfrac{\\sin{y}}{x} \\: \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x = 3\\ln 2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198045 :question/id #uuid "7a42fb45-176b-5550-a206-41a92859987b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12440" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9b1b286210d694cd3786fe38190216da503bfa3223114f86c8706f810313e33.png)\n\nThe curve $C_1$ has endpoints $A(5,3)$ and $B(1,3).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\sqrt{y^3}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{(5-x)^3}\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n through $C_1$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector  $\\mathbf{n}_1,$ as shown above. \n\n*Hint: You're given that $\\mathbf F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.$\n\n- The flux $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1, C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sqrt{y^3}\\right) + \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\sqrt{(5-x)^3}\\right) = 0+0 = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{F}$ is source-free, we know the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C_2$ is the straight line from $A(5,3)$ to $B(1,3)$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below. \n\n![](images/2a/2aeb26e58251923821d4058f886eada6a29b473ea70714b32e98c5338ddd0b28.png)\n\nNotice that both $\\mathbf n_1$ and $\\mathbf n_2$ are $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C_2,$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\,\\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_{C_2} P\\,\\textrm{d}y - Q\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t).$\n\nParametrizing $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5\\,i+3\\,j+t\\cdot [(i+3\\,j)-(5\\,i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(5-4t)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}=\\sqrt{(3)^{3}}=3\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\sqrt{(5-x)^{3}}=\\sqrt{(5-(5-4t))^{3}}=8\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-4t)=-4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3\\sqrt{3})\\cdot (0)-(8\\sqrt{{t}^{3}})\\cdot (-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}32{t}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{64{t}^{5/2}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{64(1)^{5/2}}{5})-(\\frac{(64(0)^{5/2}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1\\, \\textrm{d}s = \\dfrac{64}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198046 :question/id #uuid "8d046f27-886d-5342-8868-e2c3ae731fa5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12453" :question/problem "![](images/92/925b2f032f751e4482632c449fff35f544929f3355655c20d6701e640a7b20a3.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} =\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $\\mathbf F =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\:  z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/f5/f5a443b0bb234662c8f4a2bc156b458ac09a8db3ce3cc67933f7465a19db2ef9.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 4$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 4$ on $S_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y) \\to (r,\\theta), \\textrm d A = r\\,\\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\pi (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1.$ Therefore, for every point on $S_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0 \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
